[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m p->verts[-1] == (A.max.y, bitcast<float>(p.count) /* == 4 */)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust reproduces this with a `#[repr(C)]` wrapper whose first field occupies
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the 4 bytes preceding the `c2Poly`, initialised to `A.max.y`. Verified over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ~100 000 randomized inputs including non-finite AABBs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Irreproducible UB: `c2GJK`'s uninitialized `c2Proxy`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c2MakeProxy` has **no** `C2_TYPE_POLY` case, so for a poly it leaves the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caller's `c2Proxy` completely unwritten — and `c2CapsuletoPolyManifold` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c2GJK(&A, C2_TYPE_CAPSULE, 0, B, C2_TYPE_POLY, ...)`. `c2GJK` declares
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c2Proxy pA, pB;` as plain locals, so the C then **reads uninitialized stack**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is on the live path of `c2AABBtoCapsuleManifold`, `c2Collide(AABB,CAPSULE)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `omni_manifold(AABB,CAPSULE)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Measured behaviour of the C library:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stack region zeroed → returns exactly what an all-zero proxy produces;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stack region dirty → returns garbage, and **segfaults** when the leftover
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `pB.count` is large (`c2Support` then walks off the array). Reproduced with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   standalone C driver.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There is therefore no Rust implementation that can match the C for arbitrary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m call histories. The Rust zero-initializes both proxies, which is the only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproducible behaviour, and the differential tests establish that precondition
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by zeroing the stack region before every call (`common::scrub_stack`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/phase_c_errors.rs::row64_non_finite_inputs` additionally proves that any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m residual divergence on this path is the C's own inconsistency: it re-runs the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same shapes through `c2AABBtoCapsuleManifold` (no `malloc` between the scrub and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the read) and requires the C's two answers to differ before tolerating anything.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## NaN payload
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x86 `addss` / `mulss` return the **destination** operand, quieted, when an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operand is NaN. In `c2Dot`'s `a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y`, gcc `-O0` makes the *y* term
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the destination of the final `addss`; LLVM `-O3` makes the *x* term the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m destination so it can return in `%xmm0`. Reordering the Rust source does not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m change this — LLVM canonicalises commutative `fadd` operands (verified by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disassembly).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Consequence: when a caller passes in **two NaNs with different payloads**, a NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result can carry a different sign bit than the C's. Everything else is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit-identical, including `±inf`, `±0.0`, subnormals and all internally generated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaNs (x86 produces `0xffc00000` for every invalid operation, so both libraries
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m agree on those). The tests enforce exactly this: `feq` (strict bit equality)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m everywhere except the NaN-input tests, which use `feq_nan` — strict bit equality
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unless *both* values are NaN.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
